  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nTonight - Wednesday Morning: Strong to Severe Storms Late Tonight\n\nA shortwave trough lifting northward out of Nebraska this \nafternoon will spark showers and storms across the northern \nGreat Plains before growing upscale into an MCS that moves \neastward towards the Upper Mississippi River Valley. MUCAPE and \neffective shear are expected to be supportive of strong to \nsevere convection with this MCS generally 1500-2500 J/kg and \n35-45kts, respectively though the majority of the effective \nshear occurs within the 0-3km layer. Thinking the MCS will be \nstrongest across our Minnesota counties though some stronger \nupdrafts may continue into Wisconsin especially with support \nfrom the nocturnal low level jet. Storms should largely be \nelevated given the stable near surface layer, mitigating the \ntornado threat, and skinny CAPE profiles should limit hail \ngrowth such that the main threat is damaging winds as DCAPE \nvalues exceed 750 J/kg. \n\nPlacement and timing of the MCS is a bit challenging with many \ndifferent CAM solutions and quite a bit of run to run \nvariability. As of right now, arrival of the MCS to the I-35 \ncorridor looks to be between 12-4 am, moving through our area\nduring the early morning hours. CAMs suggest it moves along and \njust north of I-90 where the MUCAPE gradient is maximized. Some\nsolutions do suggest a further northward placement may occur, \nthough these are outliers and confidence is highest in the near \nI-90 solution, especially with a southward trend noted in the \n01.12z HREF as compared to 12-24 hours ago. \n\nAnother aspect to watch is rainfall amounts due to PWATs of \n1.5-1.6 inches, above the 90th percentile of climatology, and \nthe skinny CAPE profiles. Probabilities for rainfall amounts \nexceeding 1 inch are 20-40% in the 01.12z HREF though locally \nhigher amounts of 2+ inches are possible per the 01.12z HREF \nLPMM. Fortunately the system will be progressive and areas along\nand north of I-90 have also been fairly dry meaning the soil \nwill be able to handle even the higher end rainfall amounts. \n\nWednesday Afternoon - Thursday Morning: Afternoon and Overnight\nStorms\n\nAfter the morning's MCS, another warm and humid day is in store\nfor Wednesday while a quasi-stationary frontal boundary remains\nsituated across our southern counties through the afternoon. \nGiven the warm and humid conditions, instability, albeit capped,\nbuilds across the region in the vicinity of the boundary with \nMUCAPE values of 2000-3000 J/kg. While forcing is weak, \nsomewhat nebulous, and the low levels remain capped some \nconvective development appears possible during the afternoon \ngiven warm air advection and moisture transport into the \nboundary. This potential is low however, generally 10-20%. \nAI/ML guidance does suggest some potential for a stronger storm \nexists due to the instability present though this is a very low \nchance (<5%) with the more favorable environment existing to \nour east.\n\nAnother MCS is possible Wednesday night into Thursday morning \nas the 850hPa nocturnal low level jet intensifies again. Can't \nrule out some stronger storms, mainly north of I-90 as the \nsurface boundary begins to lift northward, with MUCAPE values of\n1500-2500 J/kg remaining across the region although shear is \nless favorable for strong to severe convection as an upper level\njet streak propagates eastward. Could see another round of \nrainfall to around 1/2 inch with probabilities for greater than \n1/2 inch in the 01.00z LREF around 20-30%. \n\nThursday Afternoon - Sunday: Daily Storm Chances and Warm\n\nAdditional shortwaves traverse the northern extent of a \nbuilding upper ridge through the remainder of the week into this\nweekend. Daily chances for showers and storms exist with these \nwaves, ranging from 20-60% in the 01.13z NBM, but overall \ndetails are not clear. Warm temperatures also continue through \nSaturday with the NBM mean suggesting highs should be in the \nmid to upper 80s though current trends suggest a bit of a cool \ndown is in store for Sunday with highs in the upper 70s to low \n80s.",
